Subject: Resume after a Windows arm64 SEH fault through CONTEXT::Pc

mdbx_simple_SEH() recovers from a fault on the memory map (an access violation
or EXCEPTION_IN_PAGE_ERROR) by pointing the saved instruction pointer at a
recovery address and continuing. It picks the CONTEXT field by word size:
`Rip` under _WIN64, `Eip` otherwise. Windows arm64 also defines _WIN64, but
its CONTEXT has no `Rip` -- the program counter is `Pc` -- so mdbx.c does not
compile there ("no member named 'Rip' in 'struct _CONTEXT'"). R-universe
builds a Windows arm64 binary, and that build fails on this line.

The guard now chooses by architecture: `Pc` on arm64, then `Rip` on other
_WIN64 targets, then `Eip`. Generated code is unchanged on x86 and x86_64.

Caveat: this makes arm64 compile; it does not establish that the handler is
reached there. Whether the recovery path works on arm64 Windows is untested.
At worst a fault on the map aborts instead of being recovered, which is what
would happen without the handler.

--- a/src/vendor/libmdbx/mdbx.c
+++ b/src/vendor/libmdbx/mdbx.c
@@ -29898,7 +29898,9 @@
       if (seh->Registration.Handler == mdbx_simple_SEH &&
           (seh->filter == pExceptionRecord->ExceptionCode ||
            (seh->filter == EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION && pExceptionRecord->ExceptionCode == EXCEPTION_IN_PAGE_ERROR))) {
-#ifdef _WIN64
+#if defined(_M_ARM64) || defined(__aarch64__)
+        pContextRecord->Pc = (uintptr_t)seh->recovery;
+#elif defined(_WIN64)
         pContextRecord->Rip = (uintptr_t)seh->recovery;
 #else
         pContextRecord->Eip = (uintptr_t)seh->recovery;
